hundreds of migrants stuck at the border  between belarus and poland are to spend  another cold night without shelter amid  a bitter standoff between the two sides  belarus says it will not stop migrants  trying to cross its borders into eu  territory but eu member poland is  refusing to let the migrants in and has  warned of an armed escalation if the  situation continues  people caught in the middle as tension  builds between poland and belarus  on the belarus side of the border  hundreds of migrants are camped out in  the freezing cold watched over by polish  police and soldiers across the barbed  wire  poland's prime minister matteo  schmoyowski visited troops massed along  the border he said belarus was using the  migrants as quote live shields in a new  kind of hybrid warfare  poland has closed an official border  crossing from belarus  in a tweet moroviatsky said today the  stability and security of the entire eu  is at stake this hybrid attack of  lukashenko's regime is aimed at all of  us we will not be intimidated and we  will defend peace in europe with our  partners from nato and the eu  the eu accuses belarus of flying  migrants into minsk then helping them  get to the borders with its eu  neighbours in an effort to destabilize  the block  belarus denies this blaming poland for  escalating the crisis and using  excessive force to push back migrants  belarusian leader alexander lukashenko  says he does not want an armed  confrontation but nor will he back down  we know our place but we will not kneel  poland has declared a state of emergency  along the border  journalists and humanitarian groups are  barred from the area  on the other side only belarus  state-aligned media has access  the migrants have mainly come from the  middle east many fleeing war or poverty  they want to head further west for a  better future in europe  human rights activists say food and  medicine are in short supply and  temperatures are dipping below freezing  a humanitarian crisis unfolding as the  threat of conflict rises  well dw correspondent marina strauss is  reporting from just outside poland's  border exclusion zone from which  independent journalists are banned  here it seems very quiet but of course  we don't really know what's going on  inside the exclusion zone and what's  going on at the border because we cannot  uh verify what's happening and there is  a belarusian journalist an independent  one who is tweeting relatively often who  has been tweeting during the day and he  says that it's relatively calm at the  border and we've seen the pictures of  the tens of thousands of migrants  camping at the border we also know that  there are migrants in the woods here at  the polish side  it's freezing cold  i'm wearing the three kinds of jackets i  imagine that it must be really hard to  to stick out here during the night right  now  give us a sense of the mood there marina  uh polish people are a bit um  like  split some say yes we of course we have  to protect the polish border which is  also the eu's external border so we  cannot let everyone in here um and we  cannot also bow to the pressure of  the of the lukashenko of belarus who  sends the migrants here to our country  but there are also of course a lot of  polish people who  say we have to do something about this  we cannot leave the people like women  children young people in the cold and  just not not act and here in the region  there are also many people who help  migrants and refugees  all right thank you for that marina  strauss near the border between poland  and belarus  well dw correspondent terry schultz in  brussels says poland's president has  been speaking with nato about this tense  situation  this is is not a situation that nato  would would physically get involved in  uh the secretary general sends out  regular messages of support for poland  latvia and lithuania all of which are  our nato members um but just today the  polish president called nato chief  stoltenberg to um basically give him an  update on the situation on the  escalation and to warn him that you know  be ready because if this doesn't um if  this doesn't simmer down then this could  be a problem for you for nato and poland  is asking some specific things of nato  in addition to expecting the same of the  european union and that is that nato  uses its contacts in the middle east in  some of these governments in iraq in  qatar um in the uae to say the same  thing that the european union is going  to say and that is stop the flights  because poland very much sees the  flights as the problem if you stop these  people from getting on a plane you stop  the possibility that they get to the eu  border and you take that that uh  tool out of the hands of the lukashenko  regime so nato is being asked to use its  diplomatic contacts in the same way that  the european union is so very much um  wanting soft power used so that it will  never come to to hard power there is a  final possibility for for poland and  that is that it can call what are called  article 4 consultations and that is if  this gets so bad that poland feels this  is a threat to its national security but  a polish diplomat tells me they're not  anywhere near that quite yet
 

